Biblical Hebrew.", "newValue": "The Book of Zephaniah, authored in the late 7th century BCE, represents a critical component of the Twelve Minor Prophets in the Old Testament and Tanakh. Situated within the context of ancient Judah, Zephaniah's prophetic voice emerges during the reign of King Josiah, a period marked by significant religious reform and sociopolitical upheaval. The text addresses the impending Day of the Lord, emphasizing themes of divine judgment and the necessity of repentance, making it a crucial text for understanding the spiritual landscape of the time and its influence on later Jewish and Christian thought.

